{"title":"The Mariner","logline":"In a collapsed near-future city ruled by gangs, a hulking Newfoundland vigilante who built his heroic identity on the memory of a beloved father must rescue his sister from a crime lord's tower — and survive learning that the father he idolized was an abusive deadbeat who never died at all.","synopsis":"After the pandemic and the financial collapse of 2025 hollow out the world's urban cores into lawless no-go zones, vigilantes fill the vacuum the police left behind. One of them is THE MARINER — Billy/Will MacAngus — a pro-wrestler-sized fisherman-themed brawler in a knit cap, net cape and bandolier of shotgun shells, armed with a metal-edged oar carved with the word 'BOSUN.' Tracking an AirTag he secretly planted in his sister Rose's purse, he storms the Ruddy & Greene Building, fights his way to the top floor, and extracts her from the gangsters holding her.

In the elevator down, the rescue unravels. Rose refuses to leave: her purse contains a chip holding a blockchain password to $20 million she stole from Mr. Salvatori's gang, egged on by her ex-boyfriend Vinnie, one of Salvatori's men. Against his 'Mariner's code' of honesty, bravery and grit, Mariner is dragged back up through the building, fighting thugs on 12, 13 and 15 while flashbacks show his father teaching him to sail, fish and lift weights in golden light. On the 15th floor they recover the purse — and Rose casually reveals the truth: their father isn't dead. He's alive in Chimney Bay, was on the pogey the summer he supposedly trained his son, and spent it drunk in a friend's yard. The golden flashbacks curdle into their real versions: a drunk pissing off the side of a boat, a father punching his own son in an overgrown weed-choked backyard.

Gutted, Mariner is caught flat-footed when Salvatori — an all-white-clad crime boss with red-tinted glasses and a gold tooth — arrives with a submachine gun and two men. Asked if he's a 'big fucking hero,' Mariner answers 'No,' and lets himself be forced to his knees. With a gun to her head, Rose rebuilds him: he isn't his father's creation, she says, but a boy who took a deadbeat and invented a superhero out of him, then became one himself — honest, brave, and unwilling to back down from a fight. Mariner smiles and clenches his fists as we CUT TO BLACK.","act_structure":[{"act_number":1,"title":"The Rescue","start_scene":"EXT. CITY CENTRE - NIGHT (opening title: ruined post-collapse city)","end_scene":"INT. RUDDY & GREEN BUILDING - ELEVATOR — Rose reveals she must go back for her purse and laughs at the idea the gang is after Mariner","summary":"The Mariner assaults a gangster-held tower and rescues his sister Rose in an entirely off-screen, sound-only fight, then meets her bloodied in the elevator.","turning_points":["Opening title establishes the post-2025 collapse world where vigilantes replace police","The rescue plays out purely as sound — yells, gunfire, muzak 'Faith' — establishing Mariner's brutal capability","Mariner reveals he found her via an AirTag hidden in her purse at Thanksgiving","Rose demands they go back for the purse; her line 'You think they're after you?' inverts the rescue premise"]},{"act_number":2,"title":"Back Up the Tower","start_scene":"DING - elevator doors open on 12 — three thugs ambush them","end_scene":"INT. 15TH FLOOR — Mariner breaks open Salvatori's office door and Rose retrieves the purse","summary":"Rose and Mariner fight their way back up floor by floor while she confesses she stole $20 million in gang money, colliding with his rigid 'Mariner's code.'","turning_points":["Elevator ambush on 12; Rose overrides Mariner's plan and takes the stairs up","Rosco is interrogated and reveals the $20 million theft","Rose admits she stole the money from the gang; Mariner: 'I don't steal, Rose. It's against my code'","Golden-light flashbacks establish Dad as the source of the hero code","13th floor firefight: the flare gun, Vinnie the ex-boyfriend burns, Mariner is shot in the leg"]},{"act_number":3,"title":"The Origin Crumbles","start_scene":"INT. 15TH FLOOR — Rose asks about the 'juice' and challenges the 'dad shit'","end_scene":"Rose's speech at gunpoint; Mariner smiles and clenches his fists — CUT TO BLACK","summary":"Rose reveals their father is alive and was an abusive drunk, collapsing Mariner's identity just as Salvatori arrives armed; Rose then rebuilds his mythology on truer ground.","turning_points":["Rose reveals Dad isn't dead — he's in Chimney Bay, was on the pogey, and abandoned Billy at a friend's yard","Flashbacks are re-shown in their true, ugly form: the drunk in the boat, the punches in the weed-choked backyard","Salvatori arrives; asked if he's a hero, Mariner says 'No' and kneels without resisting","Rose, gun to her head, reframes him: he 'turned a deadbeat father into a superhero. Then became a real one'","Mariner smiles and clenches his fists — the hero chooses himself rather than inherits himself"]}],"themes":[{"theme":"The lie at the root of the origin story","description":"Mariner's entire heroic identity — his code, his costume, his oar carved 'BOSUN' — is built on a sanitized memory of a father who was in fact an abusive absentee. The script literally replays the same flashbacks twice, once idealized and once true.","evidence":["Golden-light flashback: 'Ya knows what's a hero? 'E's the one what's brave an true'","The same beach, revised: 'It's got two thumbs and 'e looks like this! Ha! Now fetch me anudder'","Backyard bench-press flashback replayed as Dad punching him among 'DISCARDED BOTTLES' and 'RUSTY WEIGHTS'","'It broke me when he died.' / 'Died!? ... he's living up in Chimney Bay with some skank.'"]},{"theme":"Chosen identity versus inherited identity","description":"The climax argues that a person is not the sum of what was done to them; Mariner unknowingly invented the good father he needed and then embodied him. His final smile is the acceptance of self-authorship.","evidence":["Rose: 'instead of following that path, he forged his own'","Rose: 'He somehow turned a deadbeat father into a superhero. Then became a real one'","Salvatori: 'Are you a big fucking hero?' / MARINER: 'No.'","Mariner kneels without resistance, then clenches his fists after Rose's speech"]},{"theme":"Codes and their cost","description":"Mariner's 'Mariner's code' — honesty, bravery, grit, never back down — is both his strength and his cage, borrowed verbatim from an abuser and repeatedly at odds with the messy reality of protecting his sister.","evidence":["'I live by the Mariner's code: honesty, bravery, and grit. A true hero never backs down from a fight'","Dad: 'don't never run from a fight. Not again... It's not what a MacAngus does'","'I don't steal, Rose. It's against my code' — while helping her recover stolen gang money","Rose: 'You know who you sound like...'"]},{"theme":"Siblings as the real family unit","description":"With the father exposed as a fraud and the mother only referenced, the brother-sister bond carries the story — surveillance-as-love from him, truth-telling and rescue-of-the-soul from her.","evidence":["The AirTag hidden in her purse: 'Just in case! I worry about--'","Rose binding his gunshot wound with a strip of her Tim Hortons uniform","Rose shielding him from Salvatori's contempt with the speech that saves him","'It's all I got, Billy!'"]},{"theme":"Civilizational collapse and DIY justice","description":"The world itself has lost its protective institutions, mirroring Mariner's loss of a protective father; both vacuum-fill with improvised, homemade heroism and crypto-fuelled criminality.","evidence":["Opening title: bank failures and commercial real estate collapse turn city centres into 'no-go zones. Vigilanties arise to do what the police couldn't'","Fallen skyscraper, burn barrels, distant sirens in the opening image","Thugs debating WaldoCoin and PonziCoin; $20 million held on a blockchain chip","Mariner's costume of fishing net, oar, bandolier and flare gun — homemade heroism"]}],"narrative_arc":"A compressed, near-real-time vertical action piece structured as an inversion: the hero completes his rescue in the first two minutes (entirely off-screen) and then must fight his way back UP the building he just cleared. The physical ascent is paired with a psychological descent — each floor gained costs him a piece of his self-mythology, until at the top he reaches simultaneously the money, the villain, and the truth about his father. The external climax (Salvatori's gun) and internal climax (the collapse of the origin story) land in the same beat, dropping the hero literally to his knees; the resolution is not a fight but a speech, with Rose reconstructing his identity on honest foundations. The script ends on the hero's smile and clenched fists — an unshown-fight bookend to the unshown fight that opened it.","genre":"Action-Comedy / Superhero Origin Drama (post-collapse dystopian)","tone":"Darkly comedic and irreverent — pulpy, blood-splattered action laced with regional Newfoundland humour and crypto-era absurdity — that turns startlingly raw and emotional in its final act.","protagonist_journey":"Billy 'Will' MacAngus begins as an unshakeable, faintly ridiculous force of nature: a man who has built himself — physique, code, costume, even the name 'BOSUN' carved into his oar — as a monument to a dead father he idolizes. Rose's revelation that the father is alive, was a drunk who beat and abandoned him, and that the 'training' summers were neglect, hollows him out mid-mission; asked by Salvatori if he is a hero, he answers 'Nobody' and 'No,' and submits to being forced to his knees without resistance. Rose's speech at gunpoint completes the arc by reframing rather than restoring the myth: he did not inherit heroism from a good man, he invented one out of a bad one and then became him. He rises not as his father's son but as his own creation — the smile and clenched fists signalling that the code is now genuinely his.","central_conflict":"Externally, Mariner and Rose must escape Salvatori's tower with the stolen blockchain key while every gunman in the building hunts them. Internally and decisively, Mariner must survive the destruction of the paternal myth that his entire heroic identity rests on — the question is not whether he can beat Salvatori, but whether there is any hero left inside him once the origin story is proven a lie.","setting_overview":"A near-future dystopia, sometime after 2025. A global pandemic emptied the skyscrapers, then cascading bank failures and a commercial real estate collapse turned downtown cores into lawless no-go zones patrolled by gangs and self-made vigilantes: burning barrels, homeless encampments, a toppled skyscraper filling an intersection, distant sirens that never arrive. The action is almost entirely confined to the Ruddy & Greene Building, a bullet-pocked 15-storey sandstone tower with luxury European SUVs outside — a vertical set of ruined office floors culminating in Salvatori's clean, dim, nightclub-like 15th-floor penthouse of Persian rugs, leather books and stolen paintings. Culturally the world is Canadian and specifically Atlantic-Canadian: Tim Hortons uniforms, 'pogey,' a McAngus Fish & Brewis delivery van, Newfoundland dialect in the flashbacks to a sunlit (and then squalid) coastline and backyard, all set against a crypto-scam economy of WaldoCoin, PonziCoin and $20 million on a chip.","confidence":0.9}
